1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Four delivered USD capture messages add up to 45 dollars.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Trace one event through the report to explain the difference.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
In explore.sql, sum amounts in the currency’s minor units.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
There are seven deliveries. Keep only USD captures.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The refund, the euro capture and the failed attempt fall outside the filter.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Delivery 1 carries event 101 for 1,200 cents.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Event 102 also carries 1,200 cents. This is a separate capture.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Delivery 3 carries event 101 again, with the same payload.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The fourth delivery adds another 900 cents.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Deduplicate by event_id. SUM(DISTINCT amount_minor) would give only 2,100.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Partition the delivery log by the business event ID.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Number each group from the highest delivery ID down.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
With identical replay payloads, keeping the first ranked row is sufficient.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
The report retains three events. The gap equals one replay of event 101.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
This is gross USD capture volume.
A net metric would need a separate refund contract.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Open the demo repository in VS Code and start the Docker Compose lab.

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Change the filter in DataGrip. Predict what happens if EUR is included.

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Restore USD and run the check. Find the demo code in the video description.
